  "text_sanskrit": "इन्द्रियाणि पराण्याहुरिन्द्रियेभ्यः परं मनः । मनसस्तु परा बुद्धिर्यो बुद्धेः परतस्तु सः ॥ ३-४२ ॥",
  "itrans": "indriyANi parANyAhurindriyebhyaH paraM manaH . manasastu parA buddhiryo buddheH paratastu saH .. 3-42 ..",
  "translation": "It is said that the senses are greater than the body; greater than the senses is the mind; greater even than the mind is Buddhi; that which is far greater than Buddhi is He. (42) \nV. In understanding the gradation meant here some links are to be supplied on the authority of the Katha Sruti (); then the order would be the senses are greater than the body; greater than the senses are the objects; greater than the objects is the mind ; greater than the mind is Buddhi; greater than Buddhi is Mahat Atman ; Greater than Mahat Atman is Avyakta; and far greater than Avyakta is He.\nHe is the Paramatmanan, the supreme Lord. The significance of this chain is this : the deities presiding over the objects are superior in rank to those that preside over the senses and so on. (42) \nA. Having thus taught that the Paramatman is the object to be known, and He is the post exalted and should be perceived over the ascending series of rank described above, the Lord states what that knowledge is.",
